{
 "note": "Cavendish Crescent in 1891, from the pages A. Hagues sent — now filed to the right side of the street.\n\nThe census pages say only 'Cavendish Crescent', and the record holds a North and a South at 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 and 15, so these were first prepared unfiled. Wright's Directory of Nottingham, 1894-95 (page 44, Leicester University's Historical Directories) settles it house by house. Its Cavendish Crescent south lists 1 John Manning, J.P.; 3 Kirkstall Lodge, John B. Hutchinson; 5 Robert Hutchinson; 7 Mrs Emily L. Gibson — the L16782 households, number for number. Its Cavendish Crescent north lists, between Carisbrook House and Hardwicke House, Cavendish Lodge with Alfred Goater, Henry Edward Hunt, 7 H. A. Cursham and Mr Thomas Bayley — the L16848_4 households. So L16782 is the south side and L16848_4 the north, which is also what the record's 1901 placing of Goater at 3 Cavendish Crescent North had suggested.\n\nCavendish Lodge is filed at 3 Cavendish Crescent North (#22): it stands before Hunt's number 5 in the directory's run, and the record already holds Goater and both Barnetts there for 1901. Their three records are bound by id so this adds their 1891.\n\nThe Thorpe household, with Mary Mundella visiting, is headed 13 on the south page, and the south side has no number 13 in this record or in the 1894-95 directory. That is now answered: 13 Cavendish Crescent South is the house addressed 1 South Road today, a corner property with gates onto both roads, whose converted outbuilding still carries the number as 13, The Studio. The household has been filed there. See [[thorpe-13-cavendish-crescent-south]]. Norman Bell and three servants are filed at 15 South (#44) from the page's own number, though their head is on a page not sent.\n\nNot in this file: 9 Cavendish Crescent North, the Thomas Bayley household, already complete for 1891 but split between property #9 — which is 14 Barrack Lane — and 9 Cavendish Crescent North (#25). That wants reassigning on the site, not importing.\n\nAges are as the enumerator wrote them, so a birth year worked back from one may be a year out either way.\n\n**#3058 became #4104.** A visitor of 63 in 1891, so born about 1828, entered here as *E. Barnett*. The record carries her as **Ellie Barnett (#4104), born 1827**, with 1881, 1891 and 1901. The two were joined and the old number went; this writes the binding out again.",
 "source": "1891 census, enumerator's pages transcribed by A. Hagues from images L16782_0, L16782_1 and L16848_4. Every person below appears on one of those three images.",
